{
  "id": "lowfield-cnp-eeg-alpha-test",
  "name": "Cnp occipital alpha EEG test (Cook design)",
  "category": "research",
  "evidence": "single-lab",
  "origin": "Cook, Thomas and Prato (Lawson Health Research Institute, London, Ontario)",
  "uT": 5,
  "summary": "A research probe after Cook, Thomas and Prato's EEG studies: 5 min without field, the 15-min Cnp exposure they used (here on all eight ring coils), then 10 min without field, for recording resting alpha before, during and after exposure. Compare it with a blinded sham session in counterbalanced order.",
  "notes": "Cook 2004 and 2005 exposed 20 seated people for 15 min to a Cnp-type pulsed field at ±200 µT peak, with field and sham sessions counterbalanced, and recorded alpha (8-13 Hz) at O1, Oz, O2 and P3, Pz, P4. Cook 2004 reported occipital alpha higher after exposure (parietal only marginal) and a marginal drop about 7 min later; Cook 2005 found alpha lower during exposure; Cook 2009 found opposite effects for two pulse sequences. So the direction is inconsistent and the results are single-lab. The pulse is the patent's Cnp cycle as in \"lowfield-cnp-thomas-pulse\" (838-ms bursts, 5212-ms cycle); the abstracts only call Cook's field Cnp-type, so the exact sequence is assumed. The 5-min baseline and 10-min post-exposure rest are this program's outline (long enough to cover the drop about 7 min after exposure), not periods taken from the papers. The original exposure geometry is not given in the abstracts; the eight ring coils in phase are an approximation. At most about 5 µT here against 200 µT in the original, so this copies the timing only. Left out: the EEG recording and analysis, and the sham allocation. The app's Blind option picks real or sham at random for each session and reveals it after rating, so two sessions are not guaranteed to be one of each; for Cook's counterbalanced design have a helper decide which session is the sham (field first for half the people, sham first for the others) and keep the person unaware. Fix the analysis before unblinding. Coil currents can couple into EEG leads, so test with a phantom first (Wang 2019 used saline and a cantaloupe). Not for people with epilepsy; stop on distress.",
  "setting": "Seated, eyes closed, resting, with EEG at O1, Oz, O2 and P3, Pz, P4.",
  "schedule": "Two sessions, field and sham, in counterbalanced order with the person blinded; analyse alpha (8-13 Hz) before, during and after exposure.",
  "targets": [
    "primary-visual-cortex",
    "precuneus"
  ],
  "source": "Cook et al. 2004, Bioelectromagnetics https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15042628/ ; Cook et al. 2005, Bioelectromagnetics https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15887255/ ; Cook et al. 2009, Bioelectromagnetics https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18663700/ ; US 6,234,953 B1 (Thomas, Prato, Kavaliers, Persinger), Table 1 https://patents.google.com/patent/US6234953B1/en ; Camera et al. 2015 (Cnp timing structure) https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4412122/ ; Wang et al. 2019, eNeuro (phantom controls) https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6494972/",
